# SSE-C

Encryption protects data at rest and in transit to satisfy compliance requirements and reduce the impact of unauthorized access.

## What you should know
- S3 is an object store, so the unit of storage is an object rather than a block device or file system.
- This capability helps you manage data more safely, more cheaply, or more efficiently depending on the workload.
- AWS often recommends combining this feature with IAM, lifecycle rules, encryption, and monitoring for production use.

## How it works
1. Define the storage or access pattern that needs to be managed.
2. Configure the feature at the bucket, object, or account level depending on the service behavior.
3. Verify the effect with the AWS console, CLI, or API and review logs and metrics.

## Common use cases
- Backups and retention policies
- Data sharing and application integration
- Cost optimization and governance
- Compliance and audit readiness

## Example
```bash
aws s3 ls s3://my-bucket
```

## Best practices
- Use clear naming and tagging so the data is easy to locate and govern.
- Prefer IAM and bucket policies over legacy access mechanisms when possible.
- Test configuration in a non-production bucket before rolling it out widely.
- Review billing, access logs, and object metadata after enabling the feature.

## Exam or interview notes
- Understand the difference between service behavior, bucket-level settings, and object-level controls.
- Know when this feature is appropriate and when a simpler configuration is better.
- Remember that S3 features often work best when combined with versioning, encryption, and lifecycle rules.
